A||||4||Cliff D.||What SCCA class? - opinions wanted||||1
Z||000000||Cliff D.||11-10-1999||02:11 AM||||I am going away for a few days and I would like to collect some opinions on something that is bugging me. I have two '85 GTs. One I drive daily and one I have recently gutted the interior of to begin my two year racecar project. Money is not an issue, but I can't decide if I want to build an all-out GT2 machine and be non-competitive for years and probably never win an event, or go the stock-based "improved touring" route and maybe within a couple of seasons be blowing the doors off a few competitors. What would you do? Either way, I have wanted to race for over 20 years and I will be FULLY commited.||||reg||1
Z||000001||ChadMan||11-10-1999||09:53 AM||||This is just a spectator's opinion, keep in mind, but I would definitely go IT.  I have now seen three different GT-2 Fieros run at the SCCA Nationals and not one of them was competitive.  And I doubt all three drivers were that bad.  Maybe they're saddled with too much weight?  Maybe it's hard to tune a Super Duty 4?  Who knows?<P>I bet you'd do a lot better in IT for a lot less money.  I talked to a guy at Mid-Ohio who runs an 88 Coupe in IT.  He wasn't competitive right out of the box, but did better and better all summer until his motor ate its balance shafts (I didn't ask for details).<P>Keep us informed, Cliff!  Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cliff D.||11-13-1999||09:25 PM||||Thanks for the input. The only thing that scares me about racing IT is the fact that I must use stock brake disks. You would think that SCCA would be a little more paranoid about this restriction, but I guess not. By the way, to anyone interested in IT racing, I read somewhere in the news brief section of the SCCA website that the '85-'87, 6 cylinder Fieros were being reclassified from ITS to ITA!!!! (a step down in competition)||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||11-13-1999||10:19 PM||||why don't you go run the dailly driver while you build the other...... then you get used to the type of driving. You'll find many daily drivers in scca racing. it's not that hard on the cars. actually it's easier on the cars than daily driving in most parts of the country.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ChadMan||11-15-1999||09:23 AM||||The stock brakes might not be as bad as you'd think on a racetrack!  I took a HP driving course at Mid-Ohio a few years ago in my trusty 84.  The brakes faded progressively the first few laps, but then quit fading.  At impending lockup, the pedal height was about the same as the gas pedal, so it was pretty easy to heel-and-toe.<P>I'd love to get a Fiero out on a racetrack again...guess I'll go play Need For Speed 3 some more.||||reg||1
